    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical aspects of the reaction. The synthesis of syn-propanethial-S-oxide from allicin involves a series of steps:\n\n1. Alliinase catalyzes the hydrolysis of alliin to form allicin.\n2. Allicin reacts with water to form syn-propanethial-S-oxide (S-allylcysteine sulfoxide).\n3. Syn-propanethial-S-oxide is then converted to syn-propanethial-S-oxide gas through a series of reactions involving the enzyme alliinase and other enzymes.\n4. The syn-propanethial-S-oxide gas reacts with water to form syn-propanethiol (S-allylcysteine), which is responsible for the characteristic onion smell.\n5. The syn-propanethiol can then react with oxygen to form syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which is the main culprit behind the eye irritation.\n\nAs for the eye irritation, it's primarily caused by the release of syn-propanethial-S-oxide gas, which reacts with the water in the eyes to form syn-propanethiol.",
